title: Testing shipped scripts
tags: [[Scripting the GUI]]
kind: reference
summary: The shipped scripts are tested without a GUI — a strict scripted mf for the exact commands issued, and a real throwaway daemon with only mf gui stubbed for what they do to a repository.
audience: dev


A shipped script is an orchestrator: everything it does goes through the `mf`
command — the daemon for the data, `mf gui` for the screen. Testing one end to end
would take a daemon and a GUI. Two harnesses under `scripts/` replace them; both run
the ''real'' scripts.

!! The scripted `mf`

`scripts/lib/mf-mock.sh` puts an `mf` of its own first in `PATH`. It logs every call,
answers `mf gui input` and `mf gui prompt` from queues, and serves reads from a table
of responses (a glob on the arguments, first match wins). It keeps a small store of
tags per metarecord, so the loop of `gui-tag-classify.sh` runs for real.

|!Function |!Does |
|`mock_init`, `mock_reset` |install the shim; forget calls, responses and queues |
|`mock_respond <glob> <output>` |what a matching call prints (`\t` and `\n` are expanded) |
|`mock_input <key>...` |the keys the next questions get |
|`mock_prompt <value>...` |the lines the next prompts get; `@cancel` cancels |
|`mock_queue <name> <value>...` |successive answers to the same call |
|`mock_count <glob>`, `mock_calls_matching <glob>` |what the script called |

`scripts/lib/assert.sh` has `assert`, `assert_not`, `assert_eq`, `assert_contains` and
`assert_summary`; they take the command to test as arguments.

The mock is ''strict''. It used to end with ''anything unmatched succeeds'', so the
suites could not tell a command from a typo: a renamed subcommand, a dropped flag or a
misplaced argument all passed, and broke only in front of the user — two bugs hid
there. It now answers only the shapes listed in its section 5 and fails loudly on
anything else, naming the file to extend: adding an `mf` call to a shipped script
means adding its shape there, which is the moment to check it against `mf --help`.
Its `gui input` also refuses to return a key the question did not offer.

Since every call is logged, a cost rule is an assertion: ''read once for the scope,
not once per entry'' is `mock_count 'metarecord -i * field get *'` being 0
([[Writing a GUI script]]).

!! The real daemon

The mock cannot know what a query means. `scripts/lib/daemon-fixture.sh` starts an
isolated daemon — a private `XDG_RUNTIME_DIR`, hence its own token, an empty
`--config` so that nothing is loaded, a free port — and an `mf` that stubs ''only''
`mf gui`; the rest reaches the real `mf` against that daemon.
`scripts/test-scripts-integration.sh` runs the scripts over a small repository and
asserts what the repository holds afterwards, and their exit codes. It is skipped,
loudly, when the binaries are not built.

|!Function |!Does |
|`df_available` |whether `target/debug` has the daemon and `mf` |
|`df_start` |starts the daemon; stopped and cleaned on exit |
|`df_init_repo <dir>` |inits a repository there, reconciled once, the watcher off |
|`df_hybrid <repo>` |installs the `mf` that stubs `mf gui` |
|`df_mf <arguments>` |the real `mf` on that daemon |
|`hy_input`, `hy_prompt`, `hy_query`, `hy_selected`, `hy_log`, `hy_reset` |drive and read the stub |

It is also the way to ''measure'' a script: a throwaway repository of two thousand
files, each phase timed apart.

!! The suites

One per script, `scripts/test-<script>.sh`, run by `scripts/test-shipped-scripts.sh`,
which is the `scripts` check of `scripts/check.sh`. `gui-tag-next.sh`, a pure
selector, needs no mock. Lint a script with `shellcheck -x -P SCRIPTDIR`.

For a new script, write a suite that sources the two libraries. In the response
table, put no catch-all (`path *`) before a specific line: the first match wins.
